    "body": "<!-- claude-projects-attribution: {\"github_login\":\"siainbuletin\"} -->\n_Requested by **Sia** · [project thread](https://claude.ai/code/project/chan_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW2?thread=cmsg_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW23Yd38q22bNvkAAYUumjJrU)_\n\nAdapted from Nicolas Rougier's `nano-modeline.el` and `nano-minibuffer.el` (N Λ N O, GPLv3).\n\n**What changes**\n- New `sp-nano-style` (tooling), shared by `sp-lambda-modeline` and `sp-prot-modeline` (both call `sp-nano-style-install`):\n  - **Header line** in nano's blocky style: a block with `RW` (writable, muted), `RO` (read-only, warning colour) or `**` (modified, error colour; `--` for a dedicated window), then the path of the file in bold and the project name at the right edge. Other windows get a muted block and path. Colours come from the theme (`warning`, `error`, `shadow`, inverted). The project name is cached per buffer.\n  - **Divider** between the text of the buffer and the mode line: a thin overline in the `shadow` colour on `mode-line`, `mode-line-inactive` and the lambda-line faces, set again after each theme load (`sp-nano-style-divider` turns it off). The mode line itself stays whole (nano shrinks its own to the divider; not done here, as you asked for a real mode line).\n- The header code that was in `sp-lambda-modeline` (and Prot's path construct) is replaced by this module.\n- `sp-mini-frame`: `M-x` (`sp-mini-frame-nano-commands`) opens at the bottom as in `nano-minibuffer` (full width, 5 lines, 12 px fringes, no border, background moved 8% from the theme's, a half-height blank line above the prompt). Other prompts keep the centred frame and dimming.\n- Docs, docs index and CHANGELOG updated; the docs tangle to the `.el` files.\n\n**Interpretation to confirm:** I read \"the `M-x` menu beautification\" as nano's `mini-frame` styling in `nano-minibuffer.el`. If you meant `nano-command-x` (a prompt drawn in the header line, in `nano-command.el`), say so and I will add that instead.\n\n**Not tested:** nothing was run (no Emacs in the container; the `.el` files were only paren-balanced by script). To check in Emacs 31: block colours in your themes, the divider above both mode lines, the `M-x` frame at the bottom and its background (`color-blend` is used for it).\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01MfEFZ7KDkwwcifErLfjf4X)_",
